To read the `/etc/passwd` file in PHP, you can use the following code. This example assumes you're running the PHP script on a Unix-like system where the `/etc/passwd` file is located and accessible. Keep in mind that accessing system files might require appropriate permissions.

```php
// Open the /etc/passwd file for reading
$passwdFile = fopen('/etc/passwd', 'r');
if ($passwdFile) {
    // Read the file line by line
    while (($line = fgets($passwdFile)) !== false) {
        // Process the line
        $fields = explode(':', $line);
        if (count($fields) >= 7) {
            list($username, $password, $uid, $gid, $gecos, $home, $shell) = $fields;
            echo "Username: $username\n";
            echo "UID: $uid\n";
            echo "GID: $gid\n";
            echo "GECOS: $gecos\n";
            echo "Home Directory: $home\n";
            echo "Shell: $shell\n";
            echo "---\n";
        }
    }
    // Close the file
    fclose($passwdFile);
} else {
    // Error opening the file
    echo "Error opening /etc/passwd file\n";
}
```

This script reads the `/etc/passwd` file line by line, splits each line into its constituent fields (username, password, UID, GID, GECOS, home directory, and shell), and then prints out these fields for each user. Each line in the `/etc/passwd` file represents a user and contains the mentioned fields separated by colons.

